Jeong BG <as@anysyskorea.com> wrote:
> Using netstat -a , I can see that the open socket is in CLOSE_WAIT
> state, and I don't know how to check how much time it is going to be
> in such state, and how can I 'kill' this socket. of course without
> rebooting my web server.  someone told me using 'lsof' command.  but
> My server(Tru64 4.0D) didn't display socket state.

CLOSE_WAIT is the state the TCP endpoint enters when it recieves and
ACKnowledges a FINished segment from the remote (which is how the
remote says it will be sending no more data).  It is still possible
for the local (CLOSE_WAIT) endpoint to _send_ data to the remote
(which will be in FIN_WAIT_2) and the connection will remain in
CLOSE_WAIT until the application (which has been notified of the
remote's sending of the FIN with a return value of "0" from a receive
call) calls close() or shutdown() against the socket.

rick jones
